          "reason": "Statement of Finite Dyson remainder, coefficient unitarity, and regulator counterexamples: Assume the Countable Choice axiom $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $d\\ge1$, $T>0$, $\\hbar>0$, and $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$. Let $H_0=H_0^\\dagger$ be a $d\\times d$ Hermitian matrix with entries in joules, and let $V:[0,T]\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be norm-continuous, Hermitian-valued, and in joules. Define $$V_I(t)=e^{itH_0/\\hbar}V(t)e^{-itH_0/\\hbar},\\qquad B=\\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\|V_I(t)\\|,\\qquad z=\\frac{|\\lambda|BT}{\\hbar}.$$ The coupling $\\lambda$ is dimensionless and $z$ is dimensionless. Put $S_0(t)=I$ and $$S_{n+1}(t)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}\\int_0^t V_I(r)S_n(r)\\,dr,\\qquad S(t)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\lambda^nS_n(t).$$ Then the series converges in operator norm on $[0,T]$, $S$ is the unique solution of $\\dot S The forward iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        },
