          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Take SI $e>0$ in coulombs, $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, $c>0$ in metres per second, and $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-qft-example-finite-dyson-unitarity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Its exact off-diagonal matrix-element square is $\\sin^2(g_Et/\\hbar)$; its first-order squared coefficient is $(g_Et/\\hbar)^2$, which can exceed $1$. For every positive rate $\\Gamma$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, the survival matrix-element square $\\cos^2(g_Et/\\hbar)$ is not $e^{-\\Gamma t}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
